ATCO Gas and Pipelines Ltd. is planning to relocate approximately 500m of 60.3mm diameter polyethylene gas main at the request of Tsuut&#39;ina Nation to accommodate a proposed Medical Centre currently in conflict with the existing gas main. The gas main will be installed at a depth of 0.8m - 1.1m below ground surface and will be installed primarily using horizontal diagonal drilling (HDD) and plow-in methods.

&nbsp;

Construction will include removal of topsoil, installation of gas main, back filling with original material, reclamation, and commissioning by flaring a small volume of sweet natural gas.

&nbsp;

One wetland (seasonal graminoid marsh) is located within 30m of the site project. The project will include the pre-construction wildlife sweeps.

The Pine View Golf Course proposes to demolish its clubhouse building. The building is located within the Pine View Golf Course at 1471 Blair Road in Ottawa. It has reached the end of its lifecycle and should be removed.

&nbsp;

The clubhouse is a one-storey concrete block building, with a suspected mid 1970s construction date. The building houses a dining hall/reception hall and restaurant, locker rooms, various offices, washrooms and mechanical rooms. It has a footprint of approximately 2,000 m3. There is no basement.


The building would be fully demolished. Some excavation would be required to remove the foundation. Vegetation immediately adjacent to the building may be selectively removed where required. The demolition would represent the first phase of a broader initiative, with the proponent intending to rebuild a new clubhouse in the future.

&nbsp;

The purpose of the project is to eliminate immediate and long-term health and safety risks and support the long-term stewardship and future use of the property.
&nbsp;

The proposed project would take place on lands owned by the NCC, and will be subject to a federal land use, design, and transaction approval under the National Capital Act. The NCC will make a determination under the Impact Assessment Act.


The construction area would be accessed with machines and equipment, and all work areas would be fenced accordingly. Construction would take place in the fall of 2026, outside the active nesting period for migratory birds. Construction work would follow all municipal by-laws, including those related to noise.
&nbsp;

Zagime Anishinabek is proposing a mix of both large and small format commercial tenants, medium and high-density mixed-use residential areas, and additional multi-unit residential buildings on the urban reserve of Saulteaux Junction Zagime IR No. 74-6. The proposed project is called Sage Meadow Development and will cover approximately 280 acres. Sage Meadow Development will utilize the existing systems on the property and tie in new service connections. The site is currently undeveloped agricultural land and project will be completed in phases. An existing gas station and two commercial buildings are located along the east side of the subject property. The remainder of the site consists of cultivated farmland.

&nbsp;

Phase1A will require roadwork and site preparation, trench excavation and backfill, pressure pipe domestic sewer, building services, utilities. Utilities currently identified on site are: Power lines, Buried electrical cable, buried or overhead phone cable, low pressure gas utility, buried sanitary sewer, buried storm sewer, buried water line.

&nbsp;

Approximately 1,190 m of roadways, including the reconstruction of Three Sisters Avenue to an urban collector cross-section, and intersection improvements at Dewdney Avenue and Pinkie Road, including turn lanes and traffic signals.

Star Blanket Cree Nation, Okanese First Nation, and Little Black Bear First Nation are proposing to construct a Waste Diversion Centre to serve the three member Nations.

&nbsp;

The development will include the construction of an attendant building with site equipment storage bays, along with an aggregate-surfaced pad area designed to accommodate diversion and storage bays for recyclable materials and household hazardous waste (including secondary containment). The site will also feature an elevated loading platform to facilitate transfer of refuse into waste containers. The constructed area will encompass approximately 2.5 ha.

&nbsp;

The proposed site is located within an abandoned yard site. There is no tree clearing required. Approximately 3.5 ha of topsoil stripping required, with ground disturbance activities consisting of excavation of perimeter ditching, building up the transfer pad area, and constructing the access road.

&nbsp;

Solid waste will primarily consist of packaging for building materials. Construction refuse will be confined to and collected from the site, with ultimate disposal to an off-reserve designated landfill facility. To manage surface water, perimeter ditching will be constructed around the entire site and will tie into existing natural drainage courses. Site access will be provided directly from Highway 617.

The Jacques Cartier Bridge is a vital roadway and active mobility link between Montr&eacute;al and Longueuil. To ensure user safety, maintain mobility in the metropolitan area, and preserve the durability of the infrastructure, The Jacques Cartier and Champlain Bridges Incorporated (JCCBI) plans to carry out road rehabilitation work across the entire Jacques Cartier Bridge in 2027 and 2028.

The project includes work on the Jacques Cartier Bridge, its approaches, and certain access ramps, covering a total area of around 80,000 m&sup2;.

Major work planned


	Milling and paving of sections 2 to 8 of the Jacques Cartier Bridge;
	Milling and paving of the Longueuil approach (Section 1);
	Milling and paving of the Montr&eacute;al approach (Section 9);
	Deck waterproofing work in Section 5 (&Icirc;le Sainte-H&eacute;l&egrave;ne Pavilion);
	Other replacement and installation work on the electrotechnical components;
	Other miscellaneous work (marking, drainage, etc.).


Work schedule

The work will begin in 2027 and is expected to take two (2) work seasons, namely 2027 and 2028.

Traffic management

To limit traffic hindrances, JCCBI will favour carrying out work requiring lane closures during weekend blitzes. This will involve closing certain lanes from Friday evening until Monday morning, while always keeping at least one lane open in each direction.

Nine (9) weekend blitzes are planned over the two (2) work seasons. Other short-term partial closures may also be required.

Environmental protection measures

The project includes the implementation of measures to protect the environment and limit adverse effects potentially caused by the work.

The project involves installing three 45,000-litre storage tanks at the YUL Montr&eacute;al-Trudeau Airport site for the storage of diesel, renewable diesel, and gasoline. These tanks will be used to fuel the heavy vehicles and maintenance equipment required for daily airport operations. The above-ground tanks will be located within the airport&#39;s secured area, near the maintenance garage.

&nbsp;

By establishing on-site fuel storage capacity, ADM will enhance its ability to use renewable diesel, a fuel source that generates lower greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions.

Duncan&#39;s First Nation is proposing to construct and operate a Waste Transfer Station to receive and sort the community&#39;s residential waste streams including&nbsp;large items, recyclables, residential renovation waste, tires, household hazardous materials, electronics, whitegoods, propane tanks,&nbsp;and derelict vehicles. The waste will be hauled from the new transfer station by Duncan&#39;s First Nation to third-party operated landfills.&nbsp;The transfer station will have a 30-year service life with provision for future&nbsp;expansion to accommodate community growth.&nbsp;

The Transfer Station is located in a previously disturbed borrow pit requiring clearing of second-growth vegetation. The Project will involve the construction of an elevated platform for disposal of residential waste, a designated household hazardous waste collection site with appropriate environmental protections envisioned as a set of&nbsp;modified &quot;sea-can&quot; shipping containers equipped with secondary containment, holding tanks, collection and storage areas&nbsp;for safe temporary storage of household recyclables, derelict vehicles, and bulky items, and an attendant&#39;s shelter.&nbsp;

Due to previous disturbance it is unlikely Species at Risk will be impacted. A wildlife sweep and biophysical assessment was conducted to support an initial application for review, and to support due-diligence environmental management objectives.

As part of its commitment to border infrastructure enhancements, the Canada Border Services Agency (CBSA) is upgrading or replacing select land border ports of entry (POE) to modernize its custodial facilities. Included as part of this initiative, is the Pleasant Camp POE. The new building will replace the current facility that is past its lifecycle, and will be rebuilt within the existing site footprint.

This will require the construction of new buildings, infrastructure and the demolition of existing facilities. Work will be planned gradually in order to maintain the site operations at all times throughout. Construction is scheduled to begin summer 2026 and completed summer 2029.

The activities associated with the completion of the project include:


	Conduct environmental surveys and works per Construction Environmental Management Plan(s)
	Excavation and leveling work 
	Potential management and removal of contaminated soils;
	Construction of roadworks&nbsp; 
	Construction of new buildings, and other infrastructure;
	Demolition of buildings, and other infrastructure;


&nbsp;

As part of the project planning stage, environmental assessments were completed. An Environmental Effects Evaluation performed by WSP Engineering in 2018 included a review of biophysical, socio-economic, and health effects. In 2025, Keystone Engineering was engaged to update these reviews; consequently, a biological field assessment was completed and a Construction Environmental Management Plan was developed.

Additionally, Atkins R&eacute;alis completed an Archaeological Overview Assessment in 2025 to evaluate the potential for proposed works to impact archaeological resources. The potential for the work area was identified as &quot;low&quot; and a procedure has been established in the event of a discovery.

The CBSA has engaged, and will continue to engage, all potentially impacted Indigenous communities.

As part of its commitment to border infrastructure enhancements, the Canada Border Services Agency (CBSA) is upgrading or replacing select land border ports of entry (POE) to modernize its custodial facilities. Included as part of this initiative, is the Paterson POE. The new building will replace the current facility that is past its lifecycle, and will be rebuilt within the existing site footprint.

This will require the construction of new buildings, infrastructure and the demolition of existing facilities. Work will be planned gradually in order to maintain the site operations at all times throughout. Construction is scheduled to begin summer 2026 and completed summer 2028.

The activities associated with the completion of the project include:


	Conduct environmental surveys and works per Construction Environmental Management Plan(s)
	Removal of select trees;
	Excavation and leveling work 
	Potential management and removal of contaminated soils;
	Construction of roadworks&nbsp; and parking areas
	Construction of new buildings, and other infrastructure;
	Demolition of buildings, and other infrastructure;


&nbsp;

As part of the project planning stage, environmental assessments were completed. An Environmental Effects Evaluation performed by WSP Engineering in 2018 included a review of biophysical, socio-economic, and health effects. In 2025, Keystone Engineering was engaged to update these reviews; consequently, a biological field assessment was completed and a Construction Environmental Management Plan was developed.

Additionally, Atkins R&eacute;alis completed an Archaeological Overview Assessment in 2025 to evaluate the potential for proposed works to impact archaeological resources. The potential for the work area was identified as &quot;little to none,&quot; and a procedure has been established in the event of a discovery.

The CBSA has engaged, and will continue to engage, all potentially impacted Indigenous communities. As part of the construction tender, an Indigenous Participation Plan was required, and a minimum of 5% of the total contract value will be awarded to Indigenous businesses.

Ralmax Properties Limited is proposing to install a new dock facility to allow for moorage of small harbour water taxi boats adjacent to 525 Bay Street in Victoria, B.C.&nbsp;Activities associated with the project include dredging, placement of riprap, and installation of docks (including a gangway), piles, and a marine rail.&nbsp;
&nbsp;

Dredging of seabed sediments would be completed using a derrick barge and dredged materials would be transported to an upland facility for disposal. Dredging would occur within a footprint of approximately 3000 m2 to a target dredge depth of -1 m Chart Datum. Riprap would be placed along the top of the dredge cut, including along the base of an existing sheet pile wall.
&nbsp;

The dock facility is proposed to include a timber dock structure consisting of a main walkway dock, multiple finger docks, a ramp float, and an aluminum gangway. The docks will be constructed off-site and floated to the project site. The docks will be secured with 28 steel piles 0.3 m in diameter installed via vibratory hammer from a barge.&nbsp;
&nbsp;

A steel marine rail will be installed at the landward end of the docks, supported by 10 steel support piles 0.3 m in diameter installed via vibratory hammer. The rail would provide haul-out capacity for harbour taxis for upland work.
&nbsp;

Transport Canada (TC) is reviewing the project for approval under&nbsp;the Canadian Navigable Waters Act&nbsp;(CNWA) and the&nbsp;Public Ports and Public Port Facilities Regulations&nbsp;(PPPPFR), as the proposed project falls within lands administered by Transport Canada. TC will also be issuing a licence and a lease to enable the work.

This project to repair the sanitary sewer system in Timiskaming consists of several components.&nbsp;

- Installation of a storm-water system to carry away stormwater runoff and water from building foundation drains;

- Reconstruction of existing streets to address current issues with inadequate surface drainage in the community;

- Construction of a new storm-water outfall that will discharge into the stream on the other side of Stranger Road.&nbsp;

Cedar LNG Partners LP is proposing to construct and operate a floating liquefied natural gas (LNG) processing facility and marine export terminal near Kitimat, British Columbia. The proposed project would process and liquefy natural gas to produce approximately 3&nbsp;million&nbsp;tonnes of LNG per year and include storage capacity for up to 250,000 cubic metres of LNG. The proposed project would have a lifespan of at least 25&nbsp;years.

Please note the proponent was previously known as Cedar LNG Export Development Ltd.

&nbsp;
